{
  "id": "a3i5gml",
  "title": "CHAPTER 2: GEOGRAPHY OF TAMIL NADU",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "n4jm15o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.1 Location and Physical Features"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<img alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/E94eDkf_MO2-1b7Glg42t3h6YrgCZfUSacQkrpC7AKdy2Da8oQHd-hvjo91wInbFKQK1uwfy8omWj7ygU1wiess6VoDgOspB47hcwnSwpVXrWTQabRWA5yhcb2WZGozUCUKz_Y2DXd_8Lykc45OspXi2F-lX271EZpMqz5ow6lTHGjNIB25CcHPohMqez9Hq?purpose=fullsize\" src=\"/books/Guide/TamilNadu CONSTABLE/images/image_qloxksj.jpeg\"/>Tamil Nadu is located at the southernmost tip of the Indian subcontinent. It lies between 8°4' N to 13°35' N latitude and 76°15' E to 80°20' E longitude. It is bounded by the Bay of Bengal to the east, the Arabian Sea (Gulf of Mannar) to the south and southeast, Kerala to the west, Karnataka and Andhra Pradesh to the north, and Puducherry (Union Territory) on the eastern coast.",
      "id": "dzc5013"
    },
    {
      "id": "o7rz6nx",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Total Area: 1,30,058 sq km (11th largest state in India)",
        "Coastline: 1,076 km (3rd longest in India after Gujarat and Andhra Pradesh)",
        "Capital: Chennai (formerly Madras)",
        "State Bird: Emerald Dove (Green Pigeon)",
        "State Animal: Nilgiri Tahr",
        "State Tree: Palmyra Palm (Borassus flabellifer)",
        "State Flower: Gloriosa Lily (Flame Lily / Karthigaipoo)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "e29hyck",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.2 Physiographic Divisions"
    },
    {
      "id": "y5mxuaq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. The Western Ghats"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Western Ghats (Sahyadri) form the western boundary of Tamil Nadu. Important peaks include Doddabetta (2,637 m – highest peak in Tamil Nadu and the Nilgiris), Anaimudi (2,695 m – highest peak in South India, in Kerala), and Makurti Peak (2,554 m). Key passes (ghats) include Palghat Gap (Palakkad Gap), Achankovil Pass, and Shengottah Pass.",
      "id": "fcvkcos"
    },
    {
      "id": "qjdvxx0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. The Eastern Coastal Plains"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Eastern Coastal Plains, also called the Coromandel Coast, extend along the Bay of Bengal. These plains are wider in the south and support extensive rice cultivation (Kaveri delta – 'Granary of South India'). The Northern Circars plains are at the northern end, while the Carnatic plains cover most of the coast.",
      "id": "vv8weja"
    },
    {
      "id": "tjfcy7r",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. The Deccan Plateau"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The western part of Tamil Nadu includes portions of the Deccan Plateau. The terrain is generally flat with low hills. The Dharmapuri and Krishnagiri districts fall in this zone.",
      "id": "9i1a1wk"
    },
    {
